.TH GIT-SD 1 "2026-09-10" "git-sd" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
git-sd \- manage a stacked-diff workflow with GitHub Pull Requests
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git sd
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-sd
manages a stacked-diff workflow: a linear sequence of commits on the current
branch. Each commit is referenced everywhere by its 0-based
.B physical position
in
.B git sd status
output (the "Index" column), not by hash or git-sd-id \(em physical order is
the source of truth. Commits may be grouped into a named
.I Stack Set
(1-10 alphanumeric/hyphen characters) and pushed as GitHub Pull Requests, one
PR per top-level commit plus any sub-commits folded into it.
.PP
Every command except
.B status
prints exactly one line of output on success. Errors print one or more lines
starting with "Error: " and set a non-zero exit status. A conflict during
.B move
or
.B fixup
leaves a standard git rebase in progress: resolve it and run git rebase --continue,
or run git rebase --abort to restore the original order. A conflict during
.B rebase
behaves the same way.
.PP
A commit's identity trailer (git-sd-id) is added by
.BR track ,
or implicitly by
.B assign
and
.B move
(both track any untracked commits in range before acting).
.B status
never mutates commits \(em a commit with no id yet shows Status
.RB \[oq] \(em \[cq]
(Untracked).
.PP
Commit states, in order:
.B Untracked
(\(em) \(->
.B Ready
(blank Status, has an id, no Stack Set) \(->
.B Assigned
(\(sq, has a Stack Set, no PR yet) \(-> has a PR (one of the PR-state
indicators below) \(->
.BR Merged .
.PP
PR-state indicators shown in the Status column once a commit has a PR:
state unavailable (the lookup failed; status then prints one warning), in progress, conflict/base mismatch, changes requested, CI failing, CI
running, draft, review required, approved, merged, closed (run
.B git sd status --legend
to see the exact symbol for each).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B track
Assigns a git-sd-id trailer to every commit since the base branch that
doesn't already have one. Idempotent. Not required before
.B assign
or
.BR move ,
which both self-track; useful on its own to move commits from Untracked to
Ready in
.B status
without performing any other action.
.TP
.BI "status " "[--legend] [--no-legend]"
Lists every commit since the base branch: Icon (the state's emoji, blank
header), Status (the state's word), Index, Stack Set, git-sd-id, Hash,
Message, and PR columns. Read-only. Index is
.I N
for a top-level commit or
.I N.M
for a sub-commit attached to top-level index
.IR N .
.RS
.TP
.B --legend
Force-show the Status/PR-state legend above the table (and above the
"No local commits" message or any error).
.TP
.B --no-legend
Force-hide it (default comes from git config
.BR sd.status.legend ,
unset = hidden).
.RE
.TP
.BI "assign " "<assignment>..."
Assigns, reassigns, or unassigns commits to Stack Sets by index. One or more
.I assignment
arguments, each one of:
.RS
.TP
.I <set-name>:<index-list>
assign/reassign to a Stack Set
.TP
.I :<index-list>
unassign (remove from any Stack Set)
.RE
.IP
.I index-list
is comma-separated; each item is a single physical index
.RI ( N ),
an inclusive range
.RI ( A - B ),
or a direct sub-index assignment
.RI ( N . M ,
which sets physical index
.IR N 's
sub-index to
.I M
and assigns it to
.I <set-name>
without validating that a top-level parent precedes it \(em for that
guarantee use
.B move
instead). Reassigning a commit already in one set to another moves it
implicitly. Tracks any untracked commits in range first.
.TP
.BI "move " "<source-index>:<destination-index>[.<sub-index>]"
Reorders commits and/or attaches a commit as a sub-commit. If
.I sub-index
is given, the commit is attached to the nearest top-level commit at or before
.I destination-index
in the resulting order, inheriting its Stack Set \(em that commit must
already be a top-level commit of a Stack Set, or the move fails with no
changes made. Otherwise rebuilds the affected range via real cherry-picks,
which can genuinely conflict if content depends on physical order. Tracks
any untracked commits in range first.
.TP
.BI "sync " "[<set-name> | --all]"
Synchronizes local branch state with GitHub: pushes each Stack Set's
branch(es) and creates/updates its PR(s), then registers the set's PR chain
as a GitHub Stack (once it has 2+ PRs). Also deletes the remote branches of
commits retired by
.B rebase
or
.B merge
(after retargeting surviving PRs), and dissolves a Stack Set none of whose
commits remain. Omitted or
.B --all
processes every Stack Set; a specific
.I set-name
processes only that one. After the passes it lists what changed, one line per
PR created or updated (number, Stack Set, title, URL), then the number of PRs
left unchanged. A PR's title, body and base are sent to GitHub only when they
changed since the last sync.
.TP
.BI "merge " "[<set-name>] [--upto <index>] | --all"
Submits the top-most in-range PR to GitHub's native async stack-merge API,
which atomically merges every PR up through it and retargets everything
above it onto the new base. Approval/CI gating is entirely GitHub branch
protection. GitHub only: it never touches the local branch; run
.B rebase
afterwards to drop the merged commits locally.
.RS
.TP
.BI --upto " index"
Limit the merge to the bottom prefix of the set: top-level commits with
physical index <=
.IR index .
Every group at or below the target must already be synced. Cannot combine
with
.BR --all .
.TP
.B --all
Merge every Stack Set, each up to its own top. Cannot combine with
.BR --upto .
.RE
.IP
A
.I set-name
is required unless
.B --all
is given.
.TP
.BI "rebase " "[--rollback [<n>] | --list]"
Reconciles the local branch after PRs have landed, however they were merged
(squash, merge commit, or rebase-merge). Fetches the base branch and compares
each merged PR with what landed by net content: fully represented upstream
(identical, or upstream has more) -> dropped; local has extra changes that
never landed -> replaced by one follow-up commit carrying only those changes,
kept in the same Stack Set (run sync to open its PR); local and upstream changed
the same lines differently -> a git rebase conflict on that follow-up commit.
The remaining commits are rebuilt onto the updated base. A merged PR whose
merge commit is not on the base branch aborts the command with nothing
changed. A closed PR's
commits stay on the branch but lose their tracking. Strictly local: remote
branches of finished PRs are deleted by the next
.BR sync .
Refuses to run with uncommitted changes. A backup (HEAD plus metadata) is
recorded first; a conflict during the rebuild leaves a standard git rebase in
progress (git rebase --continue, --skip or --abort).
.RS
.TP
.BI "--rollback " "[<n>]"
Restore backup
.I n
(1, the default, is the most recent): resets the branch and restores metadata
together.
.TP
.B --list
List the available backups, most recent first.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --help
Open this manual page.
.TP
.B -h
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B --agent
Print a detailed, complete command reference intended for AI agents/scripts
driving git-sd programmatically \(em includes exact argument grammar and
error conditions for every command in plain text, not just this page's prose.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Set with
.B git config <key> <value>
for the current project, or
.B git config --global <key> <value>
for all projects.
.TP
.B sd.status.legend
[true|false] Always show the status legend in
.BR status .
Default: false.
.TP
.B sd.status.columns
[string] Comma-separated column list for
.BR status .
Default: "Icon,Status,Index,Stack Set,git-sd-id,Hash,Message,PR". An unknown column name is an error.
.TP
.B sd.status.color
[true|false] Color the Status word of
.B status
by state. Default: true. Only used when standard output is a terminal, and off when
.B NO_COLOR
is set or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb .
.TP
.B sd.base-branch
[string] Upstream branch to target, as "<remote>/<branch>";
.I remote
must be a configured git remote. If unset: the "origin" remote's main branch
if origin exists, else the sole remote's main branch if exactly one remote
is configured; otherwise commands fail asking you to set this explicitly.
Useful for testing against a branch without main's approval guards.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B non-zero
on any error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.B git-sd --agent
for a complete machine-readable reference;
.B git-sd -h
for a short summary; the project's SPEC.md for the full behavioral
specification.
